content.explanation:
**The @Test annotation is the most important and feature-rich annotation in TestNG. It marks a method as a test and provides numerous parameters to control test behavior.**

**Basic Usage:**

```java
@Test
public void basicTest() {
    // Test logic
}
```

**Complete @Test Parameters:**

| Parameter | Type | Default | Description |
|-----------|------|---------|-------------|
| `alwaysRun` | boolean | false | If true, test runs even if dependencies fail |
| `dataProvider` | String | none | Name of DataProvider method |
| `dataProviderClass` | Class | none | Class containing DataProvider |
| `dependsOnGroups` | String[] | {} | Groups this test depends on |
| `dependsOnMethods` | String[] | {} | Methods this test depends on |
| `description` | String | "" | Test description for reports |
| `enabled` | boolean | true | Whether test is enabled |
| `expectedExceptions` | Class[] | {} | Exceptions test should throw |
| `expectedExceptionsMessageRegExp` | String | "" | Regexp for exception message |
| `groups` | String[] | {} | Groups this test belongs to |
| `invocationCount` | int | 1 | Number of times to run test |
| `invocationTimeOut` | long | 0 | Timeout for all invocations combined |
| `priority` | int | 0 | Test execution priority (lower runs first) |
| `successPercentage` | int | 100 | Minimum success rate for multiple invocations |
| `singleThreaded` | boolean | false | Run all methods in this class in single thread |
| `timeOut` | long | 0 | Maximum time in milliseconds |
| `threadPoolSize` | int | 0 | Thread pool size for parallel execution |
| `sequential` | boolean | false | Run methods sequentially in same thread |
| `skipFailedInvocations` | boolean | false | Skip remaining invocations after failure |

**Examples of @Test Parameters:**

**1. Test with Description:**
```java
@Test(description = "Verify user can login with valid credentials")
public void testValidLogin() {
    // Test implementation
}
```

**2. Test with Timeout:**
```java
@Test(timeOut = 5000)  // Fails if takes more than 5 seconds
public void testWithTimeout() throws InterruptedException {
    Thread.sleep(6000);  // This will cause failure
}
```

**3. Expected Exceptions:**
```java
@Test(expectedExceptions = IllegalArgumentException.class,
      expectedExceptionsMessageRegExp = ".*negative.*")
public void testNegativeAmount() {
    account.withdraw(-100);  // Should throw exception
}

@Test(expectedExceptions = {IOException.class, SQLException.class})
public void testMultipleExceptions() throws Exception {
    // Test that can throw either exception
}
```

**4. Test Groups:**
```java
@Test(groups = {"smoke", "login"})
public void testValidLogin() {
    // Part of smoke and login groups
}

@Test(groups = {"regression", "checkout"})
public void testCheckoutProcess() {
    // Part of regression and checkout groups
}
```

**5. Test Dependencies:**
```java
@Test
public void testCreateUser() {
    // Create user
}

@Test(dependsOnMethods = {"testCreateUser"})
public void testLoginWithNewUser() {
    // Only runs if testCreateUser passes
}

@Test(dependsOnMethods = {"testLoginWithNewUser"}, alwaysRun = true)
public void testCleanup() {
    // Runs even if dependencies fail
}

@Test(dependsOnGroups = {"smoke"})
public void testDependsOnSmoke() {
    // Runs only if all smoke tests pass
}
```

**6. Invocation Count and Thread Pools:**
```java
// Run 10 times with 3 parallel threads
@Test(invocationCount = 10, threadPoolSize = 3)
public void testParallel() {
    long threadId = Thread.currentThread().getId();
    System.out.println("Test in thread: " + threadId);
}

// With success percentage (at least 80% must pass)
@Test(invocationCount = 10, successPercentage = 80)
public void testWithSuccessRate() {
    // Can fail up to 2 times and still pass overall
}
```

**7. Priority Ordering:**
```java
@Test(priority = 1)
public void testCreateUser() {
    // Runs first (lowest number)
}

@Test(priority = 2)
public void testLoginUser() {
    // Runs second
}

@Test(priority = 3)
public void testDeleteUser() {
    // Runs third
}

// Without priority, methods run in alphabetical order
```

**8. Data Providers:**
```java
@DataProvider(name = "userData")
public Object[][] getUserData() {
    return new Object[][]{
        {"user1", "pass1", true},
        {"user2", "pass2", true},
        {"invalid", "wrong", false}
    };
}

@Test(dataProvider = "userData")
public void testLogin(String username, String password, boolean shouldSucceed) {
    boolean result = login(username, password);
    Assert.assertEquals(result, shouldSucceed);
}
```

**9. Single Threaded (for thread-safe classes):**
```java
@Test(singleThreaded = true)
public class ThreadSafeTest {
    // All test methods in this class run in same thread

    @Test
    public void test1() { }

    @Test
    public void test2() { }
}
```

**10. Disabled Tests:**
```java
@Test(enabled = false)
public void testNotReady() {
    // This test won't run
}

// Alternative: @Ignore annotation
@Ignore
@Test
public void testIgnored() {
    // This also won't run
}
```

**11. Combined Example:**
```java
@Test(
    description = "Complex test with multiple parameters",
    groups = {"smoke", "regression"},
    dependsOnMethods = {"setupDatabase"},
    timeOut = 30000,
    invocationCount = 5,
    threadPoolSize = 2,
    successPercentage = 80,
    enabled = true
)
public void complexTest() {
    // Test implementation
}
```

questions.answer:
**Mastering Test Lifecycle Management:**

TestNG provides configuration annotations at multiple levels, allowing you to control exactly when setup and teardown code runs.

**1. @BeforeSuite and @AfterSuite**

These run once for the entire test suite, before any tests begin and after all tests complete.

```java
public class SuiteLevelTest {

    @BeforeSuite
    public void globalSetup() {
        System.out.println("=== BEFORE SUITE ===");
        // Initialize database connection pool
        DatabaseConnectionPool.initialize();
        // Set system properties
        System.setProperty("test.env", "staging");
        // Load global test data
        GlobalTestData.load();
    }

    // Test methods...

    @AfterSuite
    public void globalCleanup() {
        System.out.println("=== AFTER SUITE ===");
        // Close database connections
        DatabaseConnectionPool.shutdown();
        // Generate summary report
        ReportGenerator.generateSuiteReport();
        // Clean up temporary files
        FileUtils.deleteDirectory(new File("temp"));
    }
}
```

**Best for:**
- Database connection pools
- Global configuration
- System properties
- Test data that can be shared across all tests

**2. @BeforeTest and @AfterTest**

These run before and after each `<test>` tag in testng.xml. If you have multiple test tags, these run multiple times.

```java
public class TestLevelTest {

    @BeforeTest
    public void testSetup() {
        System.out.println("--- BEFORE TEST ---");
        // Configure for specific test environment
        String environment = System.getProperty("env", "staging");
        TestContext.setEnvironment(environment);
        // Initialize test-specific resources
        TestDataLoader.loadForTest(this.getClass().getSimpleName());
    }

    // Test methods...

    @AfterTest
    public void testCleanup() {
        System.out.println("--- AFTER TEST ---");
        // Clean test-specific data
        TestDataCleanup.clean();
        // Generate test-level report
        ReportGenerator.generateTestReport();
    }
}
```

**testng.xml:**
```xml
<suite name="Suite">
    <test name="API Tests">
        <classes>
            <class name="APITest"/>
        </classes>
    </test>
    <test name="UI Tests">
        <classes>
            <class name="UITest"/>
        </classes>
    </test>
</suite>
```

**3. @BeforeClass and @AfterClass**

These run once per test class, before any test methods in that class and after all test methods.

```java
public class LoginTest {

    private WebDriver driver;
    private LoginPage loginPage;

    @BeforeClass
    public void classSetup() {
        System.out.println("=== BEFORE CLASS ===");
        // Initialize expensive resources
        driver = new ChromeDriver();
        driver.manage().window().maximize();
        driver.manage().timeouts().implicitlyWait(10, TimeUnit.SECONDS);

        // Initialize page objects
        loginPage = new LoginPage(driver);

        // Load test data for this class
        TestData.load("login");
    }

    @Test
    public void testValidLogin() {
        loginPage.login("valid@user.com", "password");
        Assert.assertTrue(loginPage.isLoggedIn());
    }

    @Test
    public void testInvalidLogin() {
        loginPage.login("invalid@user.com", "wrong");
        Assert.assertTrue(loginPage.hasErrorMessage());
    }

    @AfterClass
    public void classCleanup() {
        System.out.println("=== AFTER CLASS ===");
        // Clean up resources
        if (driver != null) {
            driver.quit();
        }
        // Clear test data
        TestData.clear("login");
    }
}
```

**4. @BeforeMethod and @AfterMethod**

These run before and after each `@Test` method. They are the most commonly used configuration annotations.

```java
public class ShoppingCartTest {

    private WebDriver driver;
    private CartPage cartPage;

    @BeforeMethod
    public void methodSetup() {
        System.out.println(">>> BEFORE METHOD <<<");
        // Start each test with a fresh browser instance
        driver = new ChromeDriver();
        cartPage = new CartPage(driver);

        // Navigate to starting page
        driver.get("https://example.com");

        // Log test start
        System.out.println("Starting test: " + 
            new Object(){}.getClass().getEnclosingMethod().getName());
    }

    @Test
    public void testAddSingleItem() {
        cartPage.addItem("item-123");
        Assert.assertEquals(cartPage.getItemCount(), 1);
    }

    @Test
    public void testAddMultipleItems() {
        cartPage.addItem("item-123");
        cartPage.addItem("item-456");
        Assert.assertEquals(cartPage.getItemCount(), 2);
    }

    @Test
    public void testRemoveItem() {
        cartPage.addItem("item-123");
        cartPage.removeItem("item-123");
        Assert.assertEquals(cartPage.getItemCount(), 0);
    }

    @AfterMethod
    public void methodCleanup(ITestResult result) {
        System.out.println("<<< AFTER METHOD <<<");

        // Log test result
        if (result.isSuccess()) {
            System.out.println("Test PASSED: " + result.getName());
        } else {
            System.out.println("Test FAILED: " + result.getName());
            // Take screenshot on failure
            takeScreenshot(result.getName());
        }

        // Clean up browser
        if (driver != null) {
            driver.quit();
        }
    }

    private void takeScreenshot(String testName) {
        // Screenshot implementation
    }
}
```

**5. @BeforeGroups and @AfterGroups**

These run before and after specific groups of tests.

```java
public class GroupedTest {

    @BeforeGroups("database")
    public void setupDatabase() {
        System.out.println("Initializing database connection for database tests");
        Database.connect();
        Database.loadTestData();
    }

    @BeforeGroups("slow")
    public void setupSlowTests() {
        System.out.println("Setting up slow tests");
        System.setProperty("timeout", "60");
    }

    @Test(groups = {"database", "fast"})
    public void testDatabaseQuery() {
        // Database tests
    }

    @Test(groups = {"database", "slow"})
    public void testComplexDatabaseOperation() {
        // Slow database test
    }

    @AfterGroups("database")
    public void cleanupDatabase() {
        System.out.println("Cleaning up database after database tests");
        Database.cleanTestData();
        Database.disconnect();
    }
}
```

**Inheritance of Configuration Methods:**

Configuration methods are inherited from parent classes. This allows you to create base test classes with common setup.

```java
// Base class with common setup
public abstract class BaseTest {

    protected WebDriver driver;

    @BeforeClass
    public void baseClassSetup() {
        System.out.println("Base class setup");
        driver = new ChromeDriver();
    }

    @AfterClass
    public void baseClassCleanup() {
        System.out.println("Base class cleanup");
        if (driver != null) {
            driver.quit();
        }
    }
}

// Child class inherits configuration methods
public class LoginTest extends BaseTest {

    private LoginPage loginPage;

    @BeforeMethod
    public void methodSetup() {
        // driver is already initialized from BaseTest
        loginPage = new LoginPage(driver);
        loginPage.navigateTo();
    }

    @Test
    public void testLogin() {
        loginPage.login("user", "pass");
        Assert.assertTrue(loginPage.isLoggedIn());
    }

    // Inherits @AfterClass from BaseTest
}
```

**Overriding Configuration Methods:**

You can override configuration methods, but you must call `super` if you want the parent method to execute.

```java
public class ChildTest extends BaseTest {

    @Override
    @BeforeClass
    public void baseClassSetup() {
        super.baseClassSetup();  // Call parent setup first
        System.out.println("Additional child setup");
        // Additional setup specific to child class
    }
}
```

**Practical Example: Complete Test Class with All Levels:**

```java
public class CompleteTest {

    @BeforeSuite
    public void suiteSetup() {
        System.out.println("1. @BeforeSuite");
        // Global setup
    }

    @BeforeTest
    public void testSetup() {
        System.out.println("2. @BeforeTest");
        // Test-level setup
    }

    @BeforeGroups("smoke")
    public void groupsSetup() {
        System.out.println("3. @BeforeGroups");
        // Group-specific setup
    }

    @BeforeClass
    public void classSetup() {
        System.out.println("4. @BeforeClass");
        // Class-level setup
    }

    @BeforeMethod
    public void methodSetup() {
        System.out.println("5. @BeforeMethod");
        // Method-level setup
    }

    @Test(groups = "smoke")
    public void test1() {
        System.out.println("6. @Test 1 (smoke)");
    }

    @Test(groups = "smoke")
    public void test2() {
        System.out.println("6. @Test 2 (smoke)");
    }

    @AfterMethod
    public void methodCleanup() {
        System.out.println("7. @AfterMethod");
    }

    @AfterClass
    public void classCleanup() {
        System.out.println("8. @AfterClass");
    }

    @AfterGroups("smoke")
    public void groupsCleanup() {
        System.out.println("9. @AfterGroups");
    }

    @AfterTest
    public void testCleanup() {
        System.out.println("10. @AfterTest");
    }

    @AfterSuite
    public void suiteCleanup() {
        System.out.println("11. @AfterSuite");
    }
}
```

**Output when running the smoke group:**
```
1. @BeforeSuite
2. @BeforeTest
3. @BeforeGroups
4. @BeforeClass
5. @BeforeMethod
6. @Test 1 (smoke)
7. @AfterMethod
5. @BeforeMethod
6. @Test 2 (smoke)
7. @AfterMethod
8. @AfterClass
9. @AfterGroups
10. @AfterTest
11. @AfterSuite
```

content.explanation:
**Managing Test Suites in Development:**

Sometimes you need to temporarily disable tests—because a feature isn't ready, a test is flaky, or you're focusing on a specific area. TestNG provides two ways to disable tests.

**Method 1: @Test(enabled = false)**

This parameter on the @Test annotation disables an individual test method.

```java
public class DisabledTestDemo {

    @Test
    public void testActive() {
        // This test runs normally
        System.out.println("Active test running");
    }

    @Test(enabled = false)
    public void testDisabled() {
        // This test WILL NOT run
        System.out.println("This won't print");
    }

    @Test(enabled = false, description = "Feature not implemented yet")
    public void testNotImplemented() {
        // Feature not ready - test disabled
    }
}
```

**Method 2: @Ignore Annotation**

The `@Ignore` annotation provides a cleaner, more readable way to disable tests. It can be applied at method or class level.

```java
import org.testng.annotations.Ignore;

public class IgnoreDemo {

    @Test
    public void testActive() {
        // This runs
    }

    @Ignore
    @Test
    public void testIgnored() {
        // This is ignored
    }

    @Ignore("This test is flaky and needs to be fixed")
    @Test
    public void testWithReason() {
        // Ignored with explanation
    }
}
```

**Class-Level Disabling:**

You can disable an entire test class using `@Ignore` at class level.

```java
@Ignore("Entire class disabled until feature is ready")
public class EntireClassDisabled {

    @Test
    public void test1() {
        // Won't run
    }

    @Test
    public void test2() {
        // Won't run
    }

    // All tests in this class are disabled
}
```

**Conditional Disabling with @Ignore:**

You can conditionally disable tests using the `@Ignore` annotation with a condition.

```java
public class ConditionalIgnoreDemo {

    @Ignore("${environment == 'production'}")
    @Test
    public void testOnlyInNonProd() {
        // Runs only in non-production environments
    }

    @Ignore("${browser == 'safari'}")
    @Test
    public void testNotInSafari() {
        // Won't run in Safari
    }
}
```

**Disabling via testng.xml:**

You can also disable tests from the testng.xml configuration file.

```xml
<suite name="Disable Suite">
    <test name="Test with Excludes">
        <classes>
            <class name="com.example.MyTest">
                <methods>
                    <include name=".*"/>
                    <exclude name="testNotReady.*"/>
                    <exclude name=".*Broken.*"/>
                </methods>
            </class>
        </classes>
    </test>
</suite>
```

**Disabling Groups:**

You can disable entire groups of tests.

```xml
<suite name="Disable Groups">
    <test name="Test">
        <groups>
            <run>
                <include name="smoke"/>
                <include name="regression"/>
                <exclude name="broken"/>
                <exclude name="flaky"/>
            </run>
        </groups>
        <packages>
            <package name="com.example.*"/>
        </packages>
    </test>
</suite>
```

**Best Practices for Disabling Tests:**

| Scenario | Recommended Approach | Reason |
|----------|---------------------|--------|
| Single method temporarily broken | `@Test(enabled = false)` | Simple, clear |
| Method with explanation needed | `@Ignore("reason")` | Provides documentation |
| Entire class not ready | `@Ignore` at class level | Prevents all tests in class |
| Flaky test under investigation | `@Ignore("flaky")` | Marks it clearly |
| Environment-specific tests | Conditional ignore | Automatically handles environments |
| Permanent removal | Delete the test | No reason to keep |

**Example: Comprehensive Disabling Strategy**

```java
public class TestDisablingStrategy {

    // Active tests
    @Test(groups = {"smoke"})
    public void testLogin() {
        // Critical path test - always runs
    }

    // Temporarily disabled - being fixed
    @Ignore("Fixing checkout bug - JIRA-1234")
    @Test(groups = {"regression"})
    public void testCheckout() {
        // Disabled with reference to bug tracker
    }

    // Environment-specific
    @Ignore("${environment == 'production'}")
    @Test(groups = {"integration"})
    public void testDatabaseMigration() {
        // Only runs in non-production environments
    }

    // Feature not implemented yet
    @Test(enabled = false, description = "Feature not implemented - scheduled for Q2")
    public void testNewFeature() {
        // Not implemented yet
    }

    // Flaky test - marked in reports
    @Test
    public void testFlaky() {
        // This test runs but may fail intermittently
        // Use --noncritical flag in CI to not fail build
    }
}
```

**How Disabled Tests Appear in Reports:**

In TestNG reports, disabled tests appear as **SKIPPED** with a reason.

```
===============================================
TestNG Demo Suite
Total tests run: 10, Passes: 8, Failures: 0, Skips: 2
===============================================

MyTest
  testLogin                                    PASS
  testLogout                                   PASS
  testCheckout                                 SKIPPED (Fixing checkout bug - JIRA-1234)
  testDatabaseMigration                        SKIPPED (disabled in production)
```

**Important Notes:**

1.  **`@Ignore` vs `@Test(enabled=false)`**: Both work the same way. `@Ignore` is more readable and allows a reason string.
2.  **Inheritance**: Disabling is inherited. If you disable a base class, all derived classes are also disabled.
3.  **Overriding**: You cannot override a disabled method to enable it in a subclass.
4.  **Groups**: Disabled tests are excluded from group execution.
5.  **Reports**: Always include a reason when disabling tests for better traceability.

**A Deep Dive into Execution Order and Interactions:**

Understanding the precise order and behavior of TestNG annotations is crucial for writing reliable tests. This section explores edge cases, inheritance, and complex scenarios.

**1. Basic Execution Order with Multiple Test Methods**

```java
public class LifecycleDemo {

    @BeforeSuite
    public void beforeSuite() { System.out.println("@BeforeSuite"); }

    @BeforeTest
    public void beforeTest() { System.out.println("  @BeforeTest"); }

    @BeforeClass
    public void beforeClass() { System.out.println("    @BeforeClass"); }

    @BeforeMethod
    public void beforeMethod() { System.out.println("      @BeforeMethod"); }

    @Test
    public void test1() { System.out.println("        @Test 1"); }

    @Test
    public void test2() { System.out.println("        @Test 2"); }

    @AfterMethod
    public void afterMethod() { System.out.println("      @AfterMethod"); }

    @AfterClass
    public void afterClass() { System.out.println("    @AfterClass"); }

    @AfterTest
    public void afterTest() { System.out.println("  @AfterTest"); }

    @AfterSuite
    public void afterSuite() { System.out.println("@AfterSuite"); }
}
```

**Output:**
```
@BeforeSuite
  @BeforeTest
    @BeforeClass
      @BeforeMethod
        @Test 1
      @AfterMethod
      @BeforeMethod
        @Test 2
      @AfterMethod
    @AfterClass
  @AfterTest
@AfterSuite
```

**2. Inheritance and Method Overriding**

```java
public class BaseClass {

    @BeforeClass
    public void baseBeforeClass() {
        System.out.println("Base @BeforeClass");
    }

    @BeforeMethod
    public void baseBeforeMethod() {
        System.out.println("Base @BeforeMethod");
    }

    @Test
    public void baseTest() {
        System.out.println("Base @Test");
    }
}

public class DerivedClass extends BaseClass {

    @BeforeClass
    public void derivedBeforeClass() {
        System.out.println("Derived @BeforeClass");
    }

    @Override
    @BeforeMethod
    public void baseBeforeMethod() {
        super.baseBeforeMethod();  // Optional: call parent
        System.out.println("Derived @BeforeMethod");
    }

    @Test
    public void derivedTest() {
        System.out.println("Derived @Test");
    }
}
```

**When running DerivedClass:**
```
Base @BeforeClass
Derived @BeforeClass
Base @BeforeMethod
Derived @BeforeMethod
Base @Test
Base @BeforeMethod
Derived @BeforeMethod
Derived @Test
```

**3. Groups and Dependencies**

```java
public class GroupLifecycleDemo {

    @BeforeGroups("smoke")
    public void beforeSmoke() {
        System.out.println("@BeforeGroups(smoke)");
    }

    @BeforeGroups("database")
    public void beforeDatabase() {
        System.out.println("@BeforeGroups(database)");
    }

    @Test(groups = "smoke")
    public void testSmoke1() {
        System.out.println("  smoke test 1");
    }

    @Test(groups = "smoke")
    public void testSmoke2() {
        System.out.println("  smoke test 2");
    }

    @Test(groups = "database")
    public void testDatabase1() {
        System.out.println("  database test 1");
    }

    @Test(groups = {"smoke", "database"})
    public void testBoth() {
        System.out.println("  test in both groups");
    }

    @AfterGroups("smoke")
    public void afterSmoke() {
        System.out.println("@AfterGroups(smoke)");
    }

    @AfterGroups("database")
    public void afterDatabase() {
        System.out.println("@AfterGroups(database)");
    }
}
```

**Running with both groups:**
```
@BeforeGroups(smoke)
  smoke test 1
  smoke test 2
  test in both groups
@BeforeGroups(database)
  database test 1
  test in both groups
@AfterGroups(smoke)
@AfterGroups(database)
```

**4. Multiple @Before Methods of Same Type**

When you have multiple methods with the same annotation, they run in alphabetical order.

```java
public class MultipleBeforeDemo {

    @BeforeMethod
    public void aFirst() {
        System.out.println("A - runs first");
    }

    @BeforeMethod
    public void bSecond() {
        System.out.println("B - runs second");
    }

    @BeforeMethod
    public void cThird() {
        System.out.println("C - runs third");
    }

    @Test
    public void test() {
        System.out.println("Test");
    }
}
```

**Output:**
```
A - runs first
B - runs second
C - runs third
Test
```

**5. Exception Handling in Configuration Methods**

```java
public class ExceptionLifecycleDemo {

    @BeforeClass
    public void beforeClass() {
        System.out.println("Before class");
        throw new RuntimeException("Setup failed!");
    }

    @Test
    public void test1() {
        System.out.println("Test 1 - this will be SKIPPED");
    }

    @Test
    public void test2() {
        System.out.println("Test 2 - this will be SKIPPED");
    }

    @AfterClass
    public void afterClass() {
        System.out.println("After class - still runs even after exception");
    }
}
```

**Output:**
```
Before class
After class

===============================================
ExceptionLifecycleDemo
Tests run: 2, Failures: 0, Errors: 0, Skipped: 2
===============================================
```

**6. alwaysRun Parameter**

```java
public class AlwaysRunDemo {

    @Test
    public void failingTest() {
        Assert.fail("This test fails");
    }

    @Test(dependsOnMethods = "failingTest")
    public void dependentTest() {
        System.out.println("This won't run because dependency failed");
    }

    @Test(dependsOnMethods = "failingTest", alwaysRun = true)
    public void cleanupTest() {
        System.out.println("This runs even though dependency failed");
    }
}
```

**Output:**
```
failingTest: FAIL
cleanupTest: PASS
```

**7. Parallel Execution and Thread Safety**

```java
public class ThreadSafetyLifecycleDemo {

    private ThreadLocal<Integer> threadId = new ThreadLocal<>();

    @BeforeMethod
    public void setup() {
        threadId.set((int) Thread.currentThread().getId());
        System.out.println("Setup in thread: " + threadId.get());
    }

    @Test(invocationCount = 5, threadPoolSize = 3)
    public void parallelTest() throws InterruptedException {
        System.out.println("Test running in thread: " + threadId.get());
        Thread.sleep(1000);
    }

    @AfterMethod
    public void cleanup() {
        System.out.println("Cleanup in thread: " + threadId.get());
        threadId.remove();
    }
}
```

**8. Inheritance of Groups**

```java
@org.testng.annotations.Test(groups = {"base"})
public class BaseGroupTest {

    @Test
    public void baseTest() {
        System.out.println("Base test - belongs to 'base' group");
    }
}

@Test(groups = {"derived"})
public class DerivedGroupTest extends BaseGroupTest {

    @Test
    public void derivedTest() {
        System.out.println("Derived test - belongs to 'base' and 'derived' groups");
    }
}
```

**9. Priority with Inheritance**

```java
public class PriorityBase {

    @Test(priority = 1)
    public void baseTest() {
        System.out.println("Base test - priority 1");
    }
}

public class PriorityDerived extends PriorityBase {

    @Override
    @Test(priority = 2)  // Override changes priority
    public void baseTest() {
        super.baseTest();
        System.out.println("Derived override - priority 2");
    }

    @Test(priority = 3)
    public void derivedTest() {
        System.out.println("Derived test - priority 3");
    }
}
```

**10. Complete Lifecycle with DataProvider**

```java
public class DataProviderLifecycleDemo {

    @BeforeClass
    public void beforeClass() {
        System.out.println("=== @BeforeClass ===");
    }

    @DataProvider(name = "data")
    public Object[][] getData() {
        System.out.println("  DataProvider called");
        return new Object[][]{
            {"data1"},
