**Building Blocks of Test Automation:**

At its core, a TestNG test case is simply a Java method annotated with `@Test`. But effective test methods follow principles that make them reliable, maintainable, and self-documenting.

**Basic Test Method Structure:**

```java
import org.testng.annotations.*;
import org.testng.Assert;

public class BasicTest {

    @Test
    public void testMethodName() {
        // 1. Arrange - Set up test data and prerequisites
        Calculator calc = new Calculator();
        int a = 5;
        int b = 3;

        // 2. Act - Perform the operation being tested
        int result = calc.add(a, b);

        // 3. Assert - Verify the result
        Assert.assertEquals(result, 8, "5 + 3 should equal 8");
    }
}
```

**The AAA Pattern (Arrange-Act-Assert):**

This pattern makes tests clear and consistent:

| Phase | Purpose | Example |
|-------|---------|---------|
| **Arrange** | Set up test data and preconditions | Create objects, initialize variables, prepare test data |
| **Act** | Execute the behavior being tested | Call the method, perform the action |
| **Assert** | Verify the expected outcome | Check results with assertions |

**Test Method Naming Conventions:**

Good test names serve as documentation. Here are common patterns:

```java
// Pattern 1: test[Feature][Scenario][ExpectedResult]
@Test
public void testLoginWithValidCredentialsShouldSucceed() { }

@Test
public void testLoginWithInvalidPasswordShouldFail() { }

// Pattern 2: should[ExpectedResult]When[Scenario]
@Test
public void shouldReturnUserWhenValidCredentialsProvided() { }

@Test
public void shouldThrowExceptionWhenUsernameIsNull() { }

// Pattern 3: Given[Scenario]When[Action]Then[Result]
@Test
public void givenValidUser_whenLogin_thenRedirectToDashboard() { }

@Test
public void givenEmptyCart_whenCheckout_thenShowErrorMessage() { }
```

**Adding Descriptions:**

Use the `description` parameter to provide human-readable test descriptions:

```java
@Test(description = "Verify that user can login with valid email and password")
public void testValidLogin() {
    // Test implementation
}

@Test(description = "Verify that system rejects login with incorrect password")
public void testInvalidPassword() {
    // Test implementation
}
```

**Simple Test Examples:**

**Example 1: Calculator Tests**

```java
public class CalculatorTest {

    private Calculator calculator;

    @BeforeMethod
    public void setUp() {
        calculator = new Calculator();
    }

    @Test(description = "Addition of two positive numbers")
    public void testAddition() {
        int result = calculator.add(5, 3);
        Assert.assertEquals(result, 8, "5 + 3 should be 8");
    }

    @Test(description = "Subtraction of two positive numbers")
    public void testSubtraction() {
        int result = calculator.subtract(10, 4);
        Assert.assertEquals(result, 6, "10 - 4 should be 6");
    }

    @Test(description = "Multiplication of two numbers")
    public void testMultiplication() {
        int result = calculator.multiply(4, 5);
        Assert.assertEquals(result, 20, "4 * 5 should be 20");
    }

    @Test(description = "Division of two numbers")
    public void testDivision() {
        double result = calculator.divide(10, 2);
        Assert.assertEquals(result, 5.0, 0.001, "10 / 2 should be 5");
    }

    @Test(description = "Division by zero should throw exception",
          expectedExceptions = ArithmeticException.class)
    public void testDivisionByZero() {
        calculator.divide(10, 0);
    }
}
```

**Example 2: String Utility Tests**

```java
public class StringUtilsTest {

    @Test(description = "Reverse of a normal string")
    public void testReverseNormal() {
        String input = "hello";
        String expected = "olleh";
        String actual = StringUtils.reverse(input);
        Assert.assertEquals(actual, expected);
    }

    @Test(description = "Reverse of empty string should return empty string")
    public void testReverseEmpty() {
        String input = "";
        String expected = "";
        String actual = StringUtils.reverse(input);
        Assert.assertEquals(actual, expected);
    }

    @Test(description = "Reverse of null should return null")
    public void testReverseNull() {
        String actual = StringUtils.reverse(null);
        Assert.assertNull(actual);
    }

    @Test(description = "Reverse of palindrome should equal itself")
    public void testReversePalindrome() {
        String input = "racecar";
        String actual = StringUtils.reverse(input);
        Assert.assertEquals(actual, input);
    }
}
```

**Example 3: User Service Tests with Dependencies**

```java
public class UserServiceTest {

    private UserService userService;
    private User testUser;

    @BeforeMethod
    public void setUp() {
        userService = new UserService();
        testUser = new User("john.doe@example.com", "John Doe");
    }

    @Test(description = "Create user should return user with ID")
    public void testCreateUser() {
        User created = userService.createUser(testUser);

        Assert.assertNotNull(created, "Created user should not be null");
        Assert.assertNotNull(created.getId(), "User should have an ID");
        Assert.assertEquals(created.getEmail(), testUser.getEmail());
        Assert.assertEquals(created.getName(), testUser.getName());
    }

    @Test(description = "Find user by ID should return correct user",
          dependsOnMethods = "testCreateUser")
    public void testFindUserById() {
        // First create a user
        User created = userService.createUser(testUser);

        // Then find by ID
        User found = userService.findUserById(created.getId());

        Assert.assertNotNull(found, "User should be found");
        Assert.assertEquals(found.getId(), created.getId());
        Assert.assertEquals(found.getEmail(), testUser.getEmail());
    }

    @Test(description = "Find user by non-existent ID should return null")
    public void testFindUserByInvalidId() {
        User found = userService.findUserById(99999);
        Assert.assertNull(found, "Should return null for non-existent ID");
    }

    @Test(description = "Delete user should remove user")
    public void testDeleteUser() {
        // Create a user
        User created = userService.createUser(testUser);

        // Delete the user
        boolean deleted = userService.deleteUser(created.getId());
        Assert.assertTrue(deleted, "Delete should return true");

        // Verify user is gone
        User found = userService.findUserById(created.getId());
        Assert.assertNull(found, "User should no longer exist");
    }
}
```

**Using Assertions Effectively:**

TestNG provides a rich set of assertion methods in the `Assert` class:

```java
public class AssertionDemo {

    @Test
    public void testAssertions() {
        // Equality assertions
        Assert.assertEquals(actual, expected, "Optional message");
        Assert.assertNotEquals(actual, unexpected);

        // Boolean assertions
        Assert.assertTrue(condition);
        Assert.assertFalse(condition);

        // Null assertions
        Assert.assertNull(object);
        Assert.assertNotNull(object);

        // Object assertions
        Assert.assertSame(object1, object2, "Should be same instance");
        Assert.assertNotSame(object1, object2);

        // Array assertions
        int[] expectedArray = {1, 2, 3};
        int[] actualArray = {1, 2, 3};
        Assert.assertEquals(actualArray, expectedArray);

        // Collection assertions
        List<String> expectedList = Arrays.asList("a", "b", "c");
        List<String> actualList = Arrays.asList("a", "b", "c");
        Assert.assertEquals(actualList, expectedList);

        // Fail immediately
        if (someErrorCondition) {
            Assert.fail("Test failed because...");
        }
    }
}
```

