**Maximizing the Value of Data-Driven Tests:**

Data-driven testing is powerful, but it requires careful design to remain maintainable and efficient.

**1. Test Data Design Patterns**

**Use TestData Objects:**

```java
// Good: Encapsulated test data
public class TestData {
    private String username;
    private String password;
    private boolean expected;
    private String description; // For logging

    // constructor, getters, toString
}

// Usage in test
@Test(dataProvider = "data")
public void testLogin(TestData data) {
    logger.info("Testing: " + data.getDescription());
    // Test logic
}
```

**Use Builder Pattern for Complex Data:**

```java
public class TestDataBuilder {
    private TestData data = new TestData();

    public TestDataBuilder withUsername(String username) {
        data.setUsername(username);
        return this;
    }

    public TestDataBuilder withPassword(String password) {
        data.setPassword(password);
        return this;
    }

    public TestDataBuilder expectingSuccess() {
        data.setExpectedResult(true);
        return this;
    }

    public TestDataBuilder expectingFailure(String error) {
        data.setExpectedResult(false);
        data.setExpectedErrorMessage(error);
        return this;
    }

    public TestData build() {
        return data;
    }
}

// Usage
TestData validUser = new TestDataBuilder()
    .withUsername("valid@test.com")
    .withPassword("pass123")
    .expectingSuccess()
    .build();
```

**2. Data Management Strategies**

**Separate Test Data from Test Logic:**

```
project/
├── src/test/java/
│   └── com/example/tests/
│       ├── LoginTest.java
│       └── RegistrationTest.java
└── src/test/resources/
    ├── testdata/
    │   ├── login/
    │   │   ├── valid-users.csv
    │   │   ├── invalid-users.csv
    │   │   └── edge-cases.csv
    │   ├── registration/
    │   │   └── registration-data.xlsx
    │   └── common/
    │       └── environment-config.json
    └── config/
        └── testconfig.properties
```

**Version Control Test Data:**

- Keep test data in version control alongside test code
- Use meaningful file names and directory structures
- Document data format in README files

**3. Performance Optimization**

**Use Lazy Loading for Large Datasets:**

```java
@DataProvider(name = "largeDataset")
public Iterator<Object[]> getLargeDataset() {
    return new Iterator<Object[]>() {
        private int currentIndex = 0;
        private final int TOTAL = 10000;

        @Override
        public boolean hasNext() {
            return currentIndex < TOTAL;
        }

        @Override
        public Object[] next() {
            currentIndex++;
            // Generate or load data on demand
            TestData data = generateData(currentIndex);
            return new Object[]{data};
        }
    };
}
```

**Parallel Execution Configuration:**

```java
@DataProvider(name = "parallelData", parallel = true)
public Object[][] getParallelData() {
    // Data provider that runs in parallel
}
```

```xml
<suite name="Suite" data-provider-thread-count="10">
    <!-- Suite configuration -->
</suite>
```

**Cache Frequently Used Data:**

```java
public class CachedDataProvider {
    private static List<TestData> cachedData = null;

    @DataProvider(name = "cachedData")
    public static Iterator<Object[]> getCachedData() throws Exception {
        if (cachedData == null) {
            // Load data once
            cachedData = loadDataFromExcel();
            System.out.println("Loaded " + cachedData.size() + " test cases");
        }

        List<Object[]> data = new ArrayList<>();
        for (TestData td : cachedData) {
            data.add(new Object[]{td});
        }
        return data.iterator();
    }
}
```

**4. Error Handling and Reporting**

**Descriptive Test Names:**

```java
@Test(dataProvider = "data")
public void testLogin(TestData data) {
    // Good: Test reports show meaningful data
}

// Better: Include data in test name
@DataProvider(name = "data")
public Object[][] getData() {
    return new Object[][]{
        {"Valid user", "valid@test.com", "pass123", true},
        {"Invalid password", "valid@test.com", "wrong", false}
    };
}

@Test(dataProvider = "data")
public void testLogin(String description, String username, 
                      String password, boolean expected) {
    // Test implementation
}
```

**Fail Fast vs Fail Safe:**

```java
@DataProvider(name = "data")
public Object[][] getData() {
    List<Object[]> data = new ArrayList<>();

    // Option 1: Fail fast - throw exception if data invalid
    // Option 2: Fail safe - log warning and continue

    try {
        data = loadDataFromFile();
    } catch (Exception e) {
        // Option 2: Log and continue with empty data
        System.err.println("Failed to load test data: " + e.getMessage());
        return new Object[0][0];

        // Option 1: Rethrow to fail test suite
        // throw new RuntimeException("Failed to load test data", e);
    }

    return data.toArray(new Object[0][0]);
}
```

**Detailed Logging:**

```java
public class LoggingDataProvider {

    @DataProvider(name = "loggedData")
