**4. Use Appropriate Assertion Methods**

```java
// Bad - using assertTrue for everything
Assert.assertTrue(value == 5);
Assert.assertTrue(object.equals(expected));
Assert.assertTrue(list.contains(item));

// Good - use specific assertions
Assert.assertEquals(value, 5);
Assert.assertEquals(object, expected);
Assert.assertTrue(list.contains(item), "List should contain item");

// Even better
Assert.assertEquals(value, 5, "Value should be 5");
Assert.assertEquals(object, expected, "Objects should be equal");
Assert.assertTrue(list.contains(item), 
    String.format("List %s should contain %s", list, item));
```

**5. Be Careful with Floating Point**

```java
// Bad - floating point precision issues
Assert.assertEquals(0.1 + 0.2, 0.3);  // May fail!

// Good - use delta
Assert.assertEquals(0.1 + 0.2, 0.3, 0.0001, 
    "Floating point calculation should be accurate within delta");

// Better - use BigDecimal for precise calculations
BigDecimal result = new BigDecimal("0.1").add(new BigDecimal("0.2"));
Assert.assertEquals(result, new BigDecimal("0.3"));
```

**6. Assert Collections Properly**

```java
@Test
public void testCollections() {
    List<String> actual = Arrays.asList("a", "b", "c");
    List<String> expected = Arrays.asList("a", "b", "c");

    // Check entire list equality
    Assert.assertEquals(actual, expected, "Lists should be equal");

    // Check size
    Assert.assertEquals(actual.size(), 3, "List should have 3 elements");

    // Check contains
    Assert.assertTrue(actual.contains("b"), "List should contain 'b'");

    // Check order matters
    List<String> unsorted = Arrays.asList("c", "a", "b");
    Assert.assertNotEquals(actual, unsorted, "Order matters");

    // Check content ignoring order
    Set<String> actualSet = new HashSet<>(actual);
    Set<String> expectedSet = new HashSet<>(expected);
    Assert.assertEquals(actualSet, expectedSet, "Sets should be equal");
}
```

