**12. Assertion Checklist**

Use this checklist when writing assertions:

- [ ] Does the assertion have a meaningful message?
- [ ] Does the assertion validate the behavior, not implementation?
- [ ] Is this the right assertion method (equality, true/false, null)?
- [ ] Have I considered both positive and negative cases?
- [ ] Is the test name descriptive enough?
- [ ] Does the assertion help debug when it fails?
- [ ] Have I handled floating point precision correctly?
- [ ] For collections, have I considered order and duplicates?
- [ ] Are my assertions independent of each other?
- [ ] Would someone understand why this test failed from the message?

**13. Common Assertion Anti-Patterns**

| Anti-Pattern | Example | Better Approach |
|--------------|---------|-----------------|
| **Asserting without message** | `Assert.assertTrue(result);` | `Assert.assertTrue(result, "User should be logged in");` |
| **Asserting too much** | One test with 20 assertions | Split into multiple focused tests |
| **Ignoring order in collections** | Assuming list order | Use sets if order doesn't matter |
| **Floating point without delta** | `Assert.assertEquals(0.1+0.2, 0.3);` | `Assert.assertEquals(..., 0.0001);` |
| **Empty catch blocks** | `catch (Exception e) {}` | Use `expectedExceptions` or rethrow |
| **Sleeps instead of assertions** | `Thread.sleep(5000);` | Use proper waits/timeouts |

**14. Summary: Assertion Best Practices**

```java
@Test(description = "Complete example of good assertion practices")
public void testGoodAssertions() {
    // 1. Check prerequisites with hard assertions
    User user = userService.findUser(123);
    Assert.assertNotNull(user, "Test user must exist");

    // 2. Use soft assertions for multiple validations
    SoftAssert softAssert = new SoftAssert();

    // 3. Provide context in messages
    softAssert.assertEquals(user.getName(), "John Doe",
        String.format("User %d name should be John Doe", user.getId()));

    // 4. Use appropriate assertion methods
    softAssert.assertTrue(user.isActive(), "User should be active");
    softAssert.assertFalse(user.isBlocked(), "User should not be blocked");

    // 5. Validate collections properly
    List<Order> orders = user.getRecentOrders();
    softAssert.assertNotNull(orders, "Orders list should not be null");
    softAssert.assertTrue(orders.size() <= 10, "Should have at most 10 recent orders");

    // 6. Check business rules
    if (user.getAge() < 18) {
        softAssert.assertFalse(user.canPurchase(),
            "Underage users should not be able to purchase");
    }

    // 7. Report all failures
    softAssert.assertAll();
}
```

**Key Takeaway:**

Good assertions are the difference between flaky, hard-to-debug tests and reliable, maintainable test suites. Invest time in writing clear, focused assertions with meaningful messages that will help you quickly identify and fix issues when they arise.

**Skipping Tests Intelligently:**

TestNG provides several ways to skip tests based on conditions, both in code and in testng.xml.

**1. Basic Enable/Disable in XML:**

```xml
<!-- Disable entire test -->
<test name="Disabled Test" enabled="false">
    <classes>
        <class name="com.example.DisabledTest"/>
    </classes>
</test>

<!-- Disable specific methods -->
<test name="Partial Disable">
    <classes>
        <class name="com.example.MyTest">
            <methods>
                <include name=".*"/>
                <exclude name="testNotReady"/>
                <exclude name="testUnderConstruction"/>
                <exclude name=".*Broken.*"/>
            </methods>
        </class>
    </classes>
</test>
```

**2. Conditional Skipping with Groups:**

```xml
<test name="Conditional Groups">
    <!-- Include based on environment parameter -->
    <groups>
        <run>
            <include name="${environment}"/>
        </run>
    </groups>

    <classes>
        <class name="com.example.EnvironmentSpecificTest"/>
    </classes>
</test>
```

**3. Using BeanShell for Complex Conditions:**

```xml
<test name="BeanShell Test">
    <method-selectors>
        <method-selector>
            <script language="beanshell">
                <![CDATA[
                    // Skip if running in production and test is destructive
                    if (System.getProperty("environment").equals("production")) {
                        // Check if test method has destructive annotation
                        return !testngMethod.getMethodName().contains("Delete");
                    }
                    return true;
                ]]>
            </script>
        </method-selector>
    </method-selectors>

    <classes>
        <class name="com.example.ProductionSafeTest"/>
    </classes>
</test>
```

**4. Conditional Skipping with System Properties:**

```xml
<test name="System Property Test">
    <method-selectors>
        <method-selector>
            <script language="beanshell">
                <![CDATA[
                    String browser = System.getProperty("browser", "chrome");
                    // Skip Safari tests if browser is not safari
                    if (testngMethod.getMethodName().contains("Safari")) {
                        return browser.equals("safari");
                    }
                    return true;
                ]]>
            </script>
        </method-selector>
    </method-selectors>

    <classes>
        <class name="com.example.BrowserSpecificTest"/>
