**Proven Practices for Reliable Parallel Testing:**

Following these best practices will help you achieve maximum performance while maintaining test reliability.

**1. Thread Safety Patterns**

**Use ThreadLocal for Per-Thread Resources:**

```java
public class ThreadSafeBase {
    // ThreadLocal for WebDriver instances
    private static ThreadLocal<WebDriver> driver = new ThreadLocal<>();

    // ThreadLocal for test data
    private static ThreadLocal<TestData> testData = new ThreadLocal<>();

    @BeforeMethod
    public void setUp() {
        // Initialize per-thread resources
        driver.set(WebDriverFactory.createDriver());
        testData.set(TestDataFactory.create());
    }

    protected WebDriver getDriver() {
        return driver.get();
    }

    protected TestData getTestData() {
        return testData.get();
    }

    @AfterMethod
    public void tearDown() {
        // Clean up per-thread resources
        WebDriver d = driver.get();
        if (d != null) {
            d.quit();
            driver.remove();
        }
        testData.remove();
    }
}
```

**Avoid Mutable Static State:**

```java
// BAD - Not thread safe
public class BadCounter {
    private static int counter = 0;

    @Test
    public void incrementCounter() {
        counter++;  // Race condition!
    }
}

// GOOD - Thread safe
public class GoodCounter {
    private static AtomicInteger counter = new AtomicInteger(0);

    @Test
    public void incrementCounter() {
        counter.incrementAndGet();  // Atomic operation
    }
}

// BETTER - Per-thread counter
public class BetterCounter {
    private static ThreadLocal<Integer> counter = new ThreadLocal<>();

    @BeforeMethod
    public void init() {
        counter.set(0);
    }

    @Test
    public void incrementCounter() {
        counter.set(counter.get() + 1);
    }
}
```

**2. Resource Management**

**Pool Resources:**

```java
public class ResourcePool<T> {
    private final Queue<T> pool = new ConcurrentLinkedQueue<>();
    private final int maxSize;
    private final AtomicInteger created = new AtomicInteger(0);
    private final Supplier<T> factory;

    public ResourcePool(int maxSize, Supplier<T> factory) {
        this.maxSize = maxSize;
        this.factory = factory;
    }

    public T acquire() {
        T resource = pool.poll();
        if (resource == null && created.get() < maxSize) {
            resource = factory.get();
            created.incrementAndGet();
        }
        return resource;
    }

    public void release(T resource) {
        if (resource != null) {
            pool.offer(resource);
        }
    }
}

// Usage for database connections
public class DatabaseTest {
    private static ResourcePool<Connection> connectionPool = 
        new ResourcePool<>(10, () -> DriverManager.getConnection("jdbc:mysql://localhost/test"));

    private ThreadLocal<Connection> threadConn = new ThreadLocal<>();

    @BeforeMethod
    public void getConnection() {
        threadConn.set(connectionPool.acquire());
    }

    @AfterMethod
    public void releaseConnection() {
        Connection conn = threadConn.get();
        if (conn != null) {
            connectionPool.release(conn);
            threadConn.remove();
        }
    }
}
```

**3. Test Data Management**

**Unique Data Generation:**

```java
public class UniqueDataGenerator {
    private static final AtomicLong counter = new AtomicLong(0);

    public static String getUniqueUsername() {
        long threadId = Thread.currentThread().getId();
        long sequence = counter.incrementAndGet();
        return String.format("user_t%d_s%d_%d", 
            threadId, sequence, System.currentTimeMillis());
    }

    public static String getUniqueEmail() {
        return getUniqueUsername() + "@test.com";
    }

    public static TestData createUniqueTestData() {
        return new TestData(
            getUniqueUsername(),
            getUniqueEmail(),
            "password" + counter.incrementAndGet()
        );
    }
}
```

**Data Isolation with Transactions:**

```java
public class TransactionalTest {
    private ThreadLocal<Connection> threadConn = new ThreadLocal<>();

    @BeforeMethod
    public void startTransaction() throws SQLException {
        Connection conn = Database.getConnection();
        conn.setAutoCommit(false);
        threadConn.set(conn);
    }

    @Test
    public void testDatabaseOperation() throws SQLException {
        Connection conn = threadConn.get();
        // Perform operations - they'll be rolled back
    }

    @AfterMethod
    public void rollbackTransaction() throws SQLException {
        Connection conn = threadConn.get();
        if (conn != null) {
            conn.rollback();
            conn.close();
            threadConn.remove();
        }
    }
}
```

**4. Thread Count Guidelines**

**Formula Reference:**

```java
public class ThreadCountCalculator {

    public static int getOptimalThreadCount() {
        int cores = Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors();

        // For CPU-intensive tests
        int cpuIntensive = cores;

        // For I/O-intensive tests
        int ioIntensive = cores * 2;

        // For UI tests with browsers
        int uiTests = Math.min(cores, 4);  // Browsers are memory-heavy

        // For API tests
        int apiTests = cores * 3;  // API calls are I/O bound

        // For database tests
        int dbTests = Math.min(cores, 6);  // DB connection pool size

        // Determine based on test type
        String testType = System.getProperty("test.type", "mixed");

        switch (testType) {
            case "unit":
            case "cpu":
                return cpuIntensive;
            case "api":
            case "io":
                return ioIntensive;
            case "ui":
                return uiTests;
            case "database":
                return dbTests;
            default:
                return (cpuIntensive + ioIntensive) / 2;
        }
    }

    public static void adjustBasedOnMemory() {
        Runtime runtime = Runtime.getRuntime();
        long maxMemory = runtime.maxMemory();
        long usedMemory = runtime.totalMemory() - runtime.freeMemory();
        double memoryUsage = (double) usedMemory / maxMemory;

        if (memoryUsage > 0.8) {
            System.out.println("⚠️ High memory usage: reducing thread count recommended");
        }
    }
}
```

**5. Test Design for Parallelism**

**Independent Tests:**

```java
// GOOD: Independent tests
public class IndependentTest {
    @Test
    public void testFeature1() {
        // No dependencies on other tests
    }

    @Test
    public void testFeature2() {
        // Can run independently
    }
}

// BAD: Dependent tests
public class DependentTest {
    private static User createdUser;

    @Test
    public void testCreateUser() {
        createdUser = userService.createUser(...);
    }

    @Test(dependsOnMethods = "testCreateUser")
    public void testLoginUser() {
        // Depends on createdUser - not parallel-friendly
        userService.login(createdUser);
    }
}
```

**6. Parallel Execution Checklist**

Use this checklist when designing parallel tests:

- [ ] **ThreadLocal for resources** - WebDriver, connections, test data
- [ ] **No static state** - Avoid static variables, use atomic classes if needed
- [ ] **Unique test data** - Generate unique data per thread
- [ ] **Resource pools** - Pool and limit expensive resources
- [ ] **Thread-safe assertions** - Use TestNG's thread-safe assertions
- [ ] **Thread-aware logging** - Include thread IDs in logs
- [ ] **Timeouts** - Set timeouts to prevent hangs
- [ ] **Resource cleanup** - Always clean up in @AfterMethod
- [ ] **Avoid dependencies** - Design independent tests
- [ ] **Monitor resource usage** - Watch CPU, memory, connections
- [ ] **Start small** - Begin with low thread count, increase gradually
- [ ] **Test thread safety** - Write tests that verify thread safety

**7. Common Patterns and Anti-Patterns**

| Pattern | Good/Bad | Example |
|---------|----------|---------|
| **Static WebDriver** | ❌ Bad | `public static WebDriver driver;` |
| **ThreadLocal WebDriver** | ✅ Good | `ThreadLocal<WebDriver> driver = new ThreadLocal<>();` |
| **Shared counter** | ❌ Bad | `static int counter++;` |
| **Atomic counter** | ✅ Good | `AtomicInteger counter = new AtomicInteger();` |
| **Static test data** | ❌ Bad | `static List<User> testUsers;` |
| **Generated test data** | ✅ Good | Generate unique data per test |
| **Synchronized methods** | ⚠️ Use sparingly | Can create bottlenecks |
| **Concurrent collections** | ✅ Good | `ConcurrentHashMap`, `CopyOnWriteArrayList` |

**8. Monitoring Parallel Execution**

```java
public class ParallelMonitor {

    public static void startMonitoring() {
        ScheduledExecutorService scheduler = Executors.newScheduledThreadPool(1);

        scheduler.scheduleAtFixedRate(() -> {
            Runtime runtime = Runtime.getRuntime();

            System.out.println("=== Parallel Execution Status ===");
            System.out.printf("Active threads: %d%n", Thread.activeCount());
            System.out.printf("Available cores: %d%n", runtime.availableProcessors());
            System.out.printf("Memory used: %.2f MB%n",
                (runtime.totalMemory() - runtime.freeMemory()) / (1024.0 * 1024.0));
            System.out.printf("Memory max: %.2f MB%n",
                runtime.maxMemory() / (1024.0 * 1024.0));

            // Log thread dump if suspicious
            if (Thread.activeCount() > 50) {
                logThreadDump();
            }
        }, 0, 5, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
    }

    private static void logThreadDump() {
        Map<Thread, StackTraceElement[]> threadDumps = Thread.getAllStackTraces();
        System.out.println("=== Thread Dump ===");

        for (Map.Entry<Thread, StackTraceElement[]> entry : threadDumps.entrySet()) {
            Thread thread = entry.getKey();
            if (thread.getState() != Thread.State.WAITING) {
                System.out.printf("%s (ID: %d, State: %s)%n",
                    thread.getName(), thread.getId(), thread.getState());
            }
        }
    }
}
```

