**13. Performance Metrics**

```java
// Track test execution time in CI
public class PerformanceTracker {
    private static final Map<String, List<Long>> testTimes = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    @Override
    public void onTestSuccess(ITestResult result) {
        long duration = result.getEndMillis() - result.getStartMillis();
        String testName = result.getName();

        testTimes.computeIfAbsent(testName, k -> new ArrayList<>()).add(duration);

        // Alert if test is slowing down
        if (duration > 5000) {
            System.out.println("⚠️ Slow test: " + testName + " took " + duration + "ms");
        }
    }

    @AfterSuite
    public void reportPerformance() {
        // Generate performance trend report
        for (Map.Entry<String, List<Long>> entry : testTimes.entrySet()) {
            List<Long> times = entry.getValue();
            double avg = times.stream().mapToLong(Long::longValue).average().orElse(0);
            System.out.printf("%s: avg=%.2fms, runs=%d%n", 
                entry.getKey(), avg, times.size());
        }
    }
}
```

**14. Common Pitfalls to Avoid**

| Pitfall | Solution |
|---------|----------|
| **Hardcoded environment URLs** | Use system properties or config files |
| **Shared test data** | Generate unique data per build |
| **No test categorization** | Use groups to run appropriate tests |
| **Ignoring flaky tests** | Mark as flaky and retry, then fix |
| **Not archiving reports** | Always archive test results |
| **Too many parallel threads** | Monitor resource usage, adjust thread count |
| **No quality gates** | Set thresholds for pass rate and critical tests |
| **Sensitive data in logs** | Never log passwords, tokens, keys |
| **No cleanup after tests** | Use @AfterMethod and @AfterSuite |
| **Not testing in production-like environment** | Use staging environment that mirrors production |

**15. Summary: CI/CD Success Factors**

1. **Start with fast tests** - Run unit tests on every commit
2. **Layer your test strategy** - Fast, medium, slow test categories
3. **Use groups effectively** - smoke, regression, critical, flaky
4. **Parallelize wisely** - Start with methods, then classes, then tests
5. **Set realistic quality gates** - 95% pass rate, zero critical failures
6. **Monitor test trends** - Track pass rates, execution times
7. **Make reports accessible** - Publish to web, archive artifacts
8. **Notify on failures** - Slack, email, Teams integration
9. **Retry flaky tests** - But track and fix them
10. **Continuously improve** - Review and optimize regularly
